<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pre-Socratic philosophy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">6th century BCE–5th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> · Also known as early Greek philosophy</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pre-Socratic philosophy, also known as early Greek philosophy, is the ancient Greek philosophy before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socrates">Socrates</a>, which began in the 6th century BCE with the three Milesians, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thales-of-miletus">Thales</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anaximander">Anaximander</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anaximenes">Anaximenes</a>. Its thinkers were mostly interested in cosmology and the beginning and substance of the universe, and sought explanations based on natural law rather than the actions of gods: the Milesians named water, apeiron and air as the arche of the world, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraclitus">Heraclitus</a> fire. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parmenides">Parmenides</a> of the Eleatic school claimed that only one thing exists and nothing can change, while Leucippus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democritus">Democritus</a> taught atomism. Their writings are almost entirely lost and are known from fragments and testimonia.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The first pre-Socratics lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionia</a>, on the western coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>. Ionian towns, especially Miletus, had close trade relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, whose observations of the natural world differed from those of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, and the Greeks had acquired the alphabet about 800 BCE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> conquered the Ionian towns about 540 BCE. The name &quot;Pre-Socratic&quot; was first used by the German philosopher J. A. Eberhard in the late eighteenth century; earlier, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> had called these thinkers physikoi, to distinguish them from the theologoi and mythologoi who gave the gods a large part in natural events.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Its most important feature was the use of reason to explain the universe. The thinkers shared the intuition that a single explanation could account for both the plurality and the unity of the whole, and that it would not be the direct action of the gods. Many sought the arche of things, a word that could mean origin, substance or principle. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraclitus">Heraclitus</a> held that all things are in perpetual flux and that fire was the arche. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xenophanes">Xenophanes</a> pointed out that different nations depict their gods as looking like themselves.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pythagoras">Pythagoras</a>, born on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samos">Samos</a>, moved to Croton and founded a school there; his followers held that everything consists of numbers. The Eleatics argued that nothing changes. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anaxagoras">Anaxagoras</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empedocles">Empedocles</a> answered them, and Leucippus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democritus">Democritus</a> of Abdera replied that since motion exists, the void must exist too, and that indivisible atoms move within it. Anaxagoras was accused of impiety for holding that the sun was a huge burning stone, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pericles">Pericles</a> helped him to leave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The writings of these philosophers are almost entirely lost, and their views are known from later authors. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socrates">Socrates</a> was impressed by the naturalists as a young man but turned to ethics; according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xenophon">Xenophon</a>, he thought humans incapable of comprehending the cosmos. In the sixteenth century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-bacon">Francis Bacon</a> used pre-Socratic ideas extensively. The pre-Socratics are widely regarded as the first step toward scientific thought and Western rationalism.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pre-Socratic philosophy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
